 five percent so families are facing a  real struggle uh to feed uh all their  that with you know their families and uh  pay their cloak pay for the cost of  clothing and the cost of living is is is  a huge problem at the moment  you can understand mr alvin why i'm  slightly disconcerted this this morning  i i have um the foreign secretary coming  in and telling me  after she has for many years been seen  as a tax cutting uh thatcherite uh uh  member of parliament you know the  pictures on tanks and all of that  telling me oh well sorry got to put up  taxes and now you're telling me what the  government that you support which is  actually in government and has  the ability to do this should really  implement a labor program  i don't accept as a labour program i'm  i'm a practical conservative as i said  mrs thatcher imposed windfall taxes on  the oil industry and put taxes on banks  to raise uh vital funds that were needed  during the  1980s and if i have a choice between  taxing low-income workers so if you're a  worker on 20 000 pounds you're going to  be paying an extra 90 pounds if you're a  worker on 30 000 pounds you have to pay  an extra 214 pounds with this tax that's  a huge amount of money especially with  costs in energy going up in petrol and  diesel and food and and everything else  so a much better option in my view would  be a win for tax on a big business so to  pay for that that would pay for the nhs  money that is that is needed without  hitting low-income workers  all right well look i mean  let's let's suppose that the chancellor  um  decided that what he'd written this  morning was wrong having listened to on  this program  that would be an extraordinary blow to  his authority not to mention the prime  minister's were he now to change his  mind  uh you can't possibly expect that to  happen can you  well the chancellor and the prime  minister set out their view  in the newspapers this morning i  understand that it is a very difficult  financial s